# Counting the Pieces: An Interactive Tour

Some formulas are so tidy you assume they must be true. This tour interrogates one of them — the claim that a locally dense network can cheat the classical pattern-count lower bound whenever the softening exponent $p$ falls below $\binom{n}{2}/m$ — and shows exactly where and why it breaks.

## 1. The one case that works: the single edge

For a single edge the picture is completely settled by the power-mean inequality: the bound $\|W_{K_2}\|_{L^p}\ge\rho$ survives for $p\ge 1$ and fails just below. Grab the sliders and find the crossing yourself.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The clean formula predicts a threshold of exactly $1$ here, and it is right. That is the trap.

## 2. Two disjoint edges break the formula

For the 2-edge matching the conjecture predicts counterexamples all the way up to $p=3$. But the count factorizes, $\|W_{M_2}\|_{L^p}=\|W_{K_2}\|_{L^p}^2$, so the bound $\ge\rho^2$ holds for every $p\ge1$. Confirm the empty window live:

{{interactive_demo:1}}

The demo below verifies the factorization and refutes the $p<3$ prediction numerically.

{{demo:1}}

## 3. What the formula forgot: connected components

The reachable value of a block construction is governed by one graph invariant the formula never mentions — the number of connected components $c$. The closed form $\sum_\varphi \mathrm{hom}(W_t,\varphi)=t^{D}k^{c}$ makes this exact: the exponent of $k$ *is* the component count. Validate it across patterns:

{{demo:2}}

The bridge underneath is a bijection between edge-constant colorings and colorings of components, counted by this algorithm:

{{algorithm:1}}

## 4. The corrected map

Putting it together, block constructions reach exactly $(n-c)/m$, never the inflated $\binom{n}{2}/m$. Explore the corrected threshold for any pattern:

{{interactive_demo:2}}

and see the comparator logic:

{{algorithm:2}}

## 5. The visual verdict

The single-edge transition:

{{visualization:0}}

the empty matching window:

{{visualization:1}}

and the unbounded overshoot across the matching family:

{{visualization:2}}

The moral is an old one, freshly illustrated: a formula can be beautiful, symmetric, and completely wrong. The way to tell is to count the pieces.
